53518.sol:
The given differential equation is
$\frac{d y}{d x}=(x-y)^2\begin{array}{ll}\text{ Put } & x-y=t \\ \Rightarrow & 1-\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{d t}{d x} \\ \Rightarrow & \frac{d y}{d x}=1-\frac{d t}{d x}\end{array} \begin{array}{ll}
\therefore& 1-\frac{d t}{d x}=t^2 \\
\Rightarrow & \left(1-t²\right)=\frac{d t}{d x}
\end{array}On integrating both sides, we get
 \begin{array}{rlrl}
\int\frac{1}{1-t^2} d t & =\int 1 d x \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{1}{2} \log\left(\frac{1+t}{1-t}\right) & =x+c \\
\Rightarrow \quad\left(\frac{1+t}{1-t}\right) & =e^{2 x+2 c} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{1+x-y}{1-x+y} & =A e^{2 x}
\end{array} \because$ It passes through origin.
$\therefore \quad A=1\therefore$ Required curve is
$(1+x-y)=e^{2 x}(1-x+y)$

